   "date": "2014-10-12T11:02:52Z",
   "subject": "Request for change to documentation",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi - I finally figured out that dplyr::do isn&#39;t as intuitive as I&#39;d thought.  My typical usage of an anonymous function is, e.g.<br><br><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:8pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"white-space:pre;color:rgb(127,0,85)\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:rgb(127,0,85)\">group_by(as.tbl(data.frame(aa=c(1,2),bb=c(&quot;AA&quot;,&quot;BB&quot;))),bb) %&gt;% do(function(x) {head(x,1)})\r\n</span><span>Error: Results are not data frames at positions: 1, 2</span></pre></span><br>but it seems that &quot;do&quot; really does want a data frame and not a function, since the following works:<br><br><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><table style=\"color:black;font:inherit!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important;font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;padding-left:6px;padding-bottom:8px;line-height:1.2;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;word-wrap:break-word;font-size:8pt!important;width:1183px\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;line-height:1.2;font-size:8pt!important;vertical-align:top\" align=\"left\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:8pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:11px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:12px;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:pre-wrap;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:8pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"white-space:pre;color:rgb(127,0,85)\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:rgb(127,0,85)\">group_by(as.tbl(data.frame(aa=c(1,2),bb=c(&quot;AA&quot;,&quot;BB&quot;))),bb) %&gt;% do(head(.,1))</span></pre></span>Source: local data frame [2 x 2]\r\nGroups: bb\r\n\r\n  aa bb\r\n1  1 AA\r\n2  2 BB\r\n<span>\r\n</span></pre></td></tr><tr><td style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;line-height:1.2;font-size:8pt!important;vertical-align:top\" align=\"left\"><br></td></tr><tr><td style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;line-height:1.2;font-size:8pt!important;vertical-align:top\" align=\"left\">I think this may trip up a lot of people, especially those transitioning from plyr.  I&#39;m not sure how you&#39;d<br>emphasize this fact in the documentation, but it&#39;s worth a try.<br><table style=\"width:1183px\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tbody><tr><td rowspan=\"1\" style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;line-height:1.2;font-size:8pt!important;vertical-align:top\" align=\"left\" height=\"\" width=\"1\"><br></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></table></span><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "LkqlNHj-R-4J",
   "from": "Dennis Murphy",
   "author": "0c39e60ddb38622b",
   "date": "2014-10-12T20:20:48Z",
   "subject": "Re: Request for change to documentation",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I have to say I agree with this request, as the following line does not seem to be true in general:<div>&quot;<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Times New Roman&#39;;font-size:medium\">You can use </span><code style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">do</code><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Times New Roman&#39;;font-size:medium\"> to perform arbitrary computation, returning either a data frame or arbitrary objects which will be stored in a list.&quot;</span></div><div><br></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">It&#39;s the last part of that sentence I wonder about. In my reply to another thread this morning, </font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">I created a function that returned a data frame by coercing a numeric vector to a list. This</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">was necessary because returning a numeric vector threw an error. In plyr, this works:</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">library(plyr)</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\">library(dplyr)</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\"><div>DFm &lt;- mtcars %&gt;% select(mpg, disp:vs) %&gt;% gather(&quot;var&quot;, &quot;val&quot;, -vs) </div><div><br></div></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\"><div>f2 &lt;- function(DF)</div><div>{</div><div>   pv &lt;- t.test(val ~ vs, data = DF)[[&quot;p.value&quot;]]</div><div>   means &lt;- tapply(DF$val, DF$vs, mean)</div><div>   sds &lt;- tapply(DF$val, DF$vs, sd)</div><div>   stats &lt;- c(means, sds, pv)</div><div>   names(stats) &lt;- c(&quot;mean0&quot;, &quot;mean1&quot;, &quot;sd0&quot;, &quot;sd1&quot;, &quot;p.value&quot;)</div><div>   stats</div><div>}</div><div><br></div><div>plyr::ddply(DFm, &quot;var&quot;, f2)</div><div><br></div><div># whereas</div><div><div>&gt; DFm %&gt;% group_by(&quot;var&quot;) %&gt;% do(f2(.))</div></div></font></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\"><div><div>Error: Results are not data frames at positions: 1</div></div></font></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\"><div></div><div><br></div><div>Changing the last line of f2() to</div><div>data.frame(as.list(stats))</div><div><br></div><div>and re-running the modified f2() through the pipeline works. </div><div><br></div><div>Ramming the original f2() through the pipeline should be equivalent to dlply()</div><div>but it clearly is not, so either I&#39;m missing something or the present documentation is</div><div>partially inaccurate. The error message is clear enough and I modified the</div><div>function accordingly, but the documentation suggests that I should be</div><div>able to return a list of vectors and dplyr will coerce the result into a data frame</div><div>a la plyr::ldply().</div><div><br></div><div>Perhaps my confusion is this:  the documentation is accurate as far as the *result*</div><div>produced from do(), but what must the function on which do() operates return?</div><div><br></div><div>Dennis</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></font></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Sun, Oct 12, 2014 at 4:02 AM, D Holmez <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">dfhol...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi - I finally figured out that dplyr::do isn&#39;t as intuitive as I&#39;d thought.  My typical usage of an anonymous function is, e.g.<br><br><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:8pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;word-break:break-all;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;color:rgb(127,0,85)\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:rgb(127,0,85)\">group_by(as.tbl(data.frame(aa=c(1,2),bb=c(&quot;AA&quot;,&quot;BB&quot;))),bb) %&gt;% do(function(x) {head(x,1)})\r\n</span><span>Error: Results are not data frames at positions: 1, 2</span></pre></span><br>but it seems that &quot;do&quot; really does want a data frame and not a function, since the following works:<br><br><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(225,226,229)\"><table style=\"color:black;font:inherit!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important;font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;padding-left:6px;padding-bottom:8px;line-